---
source: src/adapter/cli/args.rs
expression: "helps.join(\"\\n\\n\")"
---
$ muster --help
Run a project's dev processes in the background, and control them from any shell

Usage: muster [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  ps            List this project's processes: state, pid, uptime, restarts and usage
  up            Start this project's processes that autostart
  down          Stop every process of this project
  start         Start a process
  stop          Stop a process through its stop policy
  restart       Restart a process
  wait          Wait until a process prints a line, accepts a connection, answers a URL, reaches a state, or passes its readiness check
  pause         Suspend a process, leaving it alive
  resume        Resume a paused process
  kill          Kill a process at once, skipping its graceful stop
  logs          Print a process's output log, kept across its runs and after it exits
  attach        Show a running process in this terminal and type into it
  notices       Show this project's notices: the stack edits agents made, and failed starts, forced stops and crashes when the service runs. Never starts it
  service       Show or stop the muster service. Processes keep running either way
  add           Add a process to this project, registering the folder as a project when it is not one yet. Nothing is started
  set           Change a process's definition; a running process takes it at its next start
  rm            Remove processes from this project, stopping those that run
  config        Show or change muster's settings
  export        Write this project's processes to a stack file
  import        Load processes from a stack file into this project, registering the folder when it is not a project yet
  init          Find processes in this folder's package.json, Procfile, justfile, Makefile or Compose file and add the ones chosen
  run           Add a command to this project, start it and attach to it
  projects      List registered projects, or remove one
  note          Read or write shared scratchpad notes for the current project
  todo          List or manage the current project's shared todos
  kv            Read or write the current project's shared key-value state
  lock          List the locks agents hold on the current project, or release one
  coordination  Export or restore this project's coordination state as portable YAML
  mcp           Serve muster to agents as an MCP server over stdio (spawned as a child process by a connecting agent). Each call acts on the project containing this folder, or the one --project or $MUSTER_PROJECT names, unless it names another
  doctor        Diagnose muster's database, projects and completions
  completions   Print the shell hook that enables completions
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -p, --project <PROJECT>  The project: a registered project's name, or a folder (a value with `/`, or starting with `.` or `~`). Defaults to $MUSTER_PROJECT, then the registered project containing the current folder
  -h, --help               Print help
  -V, --version            Print version

Exit codes:
    0  success
    1  a process did not end up as asked, or the action failed
    2  the command line could not be parsed, or a question was cancelled
    3  the muster service is unavailable or runs a newer version
    4  not a project, an unknown project or process, or an invalid value or file
  130  interrupted with Ctrl-C while it waited at a terminal

With --json, results and failures print as JSON on stdout. Object keys are
snake_case, enum values are kebab-case, durations are seconds, and times are
RFC 3339 in UTC.


$ muster ps --help
List this project's processes: state, pid, uptime, restarts and usage

Usage: muster ps [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --all                List every project the muster service has loaded
  -p, --project <PROJECT>  The project: a registered project's name, or a folder (a value with `/`, or starting with `.` or `~`). Defaults to $MUSTER_PROJECT, then the registered project containing the current folder
      --commands           Add a COMMAND column with each process's stored command, so an edit made by an agent shows
      --json               Print the result as JSON
  -h, --help               Print help


