$ muster add --help
Add a process to this project, registering the folder as a project when it is not one yet. Nothing is started

Usage: muster add [OPTIONS] <NAME> -- <COMMAND>...

Arguments:
  <NAME>        Its name, unique in the project
  <COMMAND>...  The command, after `--`: one argument is a shell expression, several are joined as the shell split them

Options:
      --cwd <DIR>                 Its working directory, read from the current folder; stored relative to the project when inside it. Defaults to the project's folder
  -p, --project <PROJECT>         The project: a registered project's name, or a folder (a value with `/`, or starting with `.` or `~`). Defaults to $MUSTER_PROJECT, then the registered project containing the current folder
      --description <TEXT>        A one-line description
      --env <KEY=VALUE>           Set an environment variable, `KEY=VALUE` (repeatable)
      --autostart                 Start it with `muster up`
      --no-autostart              Leave it out of `muster up`
      --restart <POLICY>          What happens when its child exits [possible values: never, on-failure, always]
      --stop-signal <SIGNAL>      The signal a graceful stop sends [possible values: terminate, interrupt]
      --stop-grace <DURATION>     How long a graceful stop waits before it kills, such as `5s`; at most 1h
      --ready-log <TEXT>          Ready once an output line contains this text; needs --ready-timeout
      --ready-regex               Read --ready-log as a regular expression
      --ready-port <PORT>         Ready once this localhost port accepts a connection; needs --ready-timeout
      --ready-http <URL>          Ready once this localhost URL answers below 500; needs --ready-timeout
      --ready-timeout <DURATION>  How long each run may take to become ready, such as `60s`
      --json                      Print the result as JSON
  -h, --help                      Print help


$ muster set --help
Change a process's definition; a running process takes it at its next start

Usage: muster set [OPTIONS] <NAME> [-- <COMMAND>...]

Arguments:
  <NAME>        The process, as `muster ps` lists it
  [COMMAND]...  A new command, after `--`, joined as `muster add` joins it

Options:
      --command <CMD>             A new command, as one shell expression
  -p, --project <PROJECT>         The project: a registered project's name, or a folder (a value with `/`, or starting with `.` or `~`). Defaults to $MUSTER_PROJECT, then the registered project containing the current folder
      --cwd <DIR>                 A new working directory, read from the current folder
      --no-cwd                    Run in the project's folder again
      --description <TEXT>        A new one-line description
      --no-description            Remove the description
      --unset-env <KEY>           Remove an environment variable (repeatable); one that is not set is left alone
      --no-ready                  Remove the readiness check
      --env <KEY=VALUE>           Set an environment variable, `KEY=VALUE` (repeatable)
      --autostart                 Start it with `muster up`
      --no-autostart              Leave it out of `muster up`
      --restart <POLICY>          What happens when its child exits [possible values: never, on-failure, always]
      --stop-signal <SIGNAL>      The signal a graceful stop sends [possible values: terminate, interrupt]
      --stop-grace <DURATION>     How long a graceful stop waits before it kills, such as `5s`; at most 1h
      --ready-log <TEXT>          Ready once an output line contains this text; needs --ready-timeout
      --ready-regex               Read --ready-log as a regular expression
      --ready-port <PORT>         Ready once this localhost port accepts a connection; needs --ready-timeout
      --ready-http <URL>          Ready once this localhost URL answers below 500; needs --ready-timeout
      --ready-timeout <DURATION>  How long each run may take to become ready, such as `60s`
      --json                      Print the result as JSON
  -h, --help                      Print help


